---
import StandardsLayout from "../../components/StandardsLayout.astro";
import CitationBlock from "../../components/CitationBlock.astro";
import SectionBlock from "../../components/SectionBlock.astro";
import { derivePublicationMetadata } from "../../utils/publication";

const pageTitle = "EU AI Act";
const pageDescription =
  "An Ethotechnics critique of the EU AI Act: most high-risk systems are self-assessed, their authority does not lapse, and a challenge need not reach them.";
const permalink = "/standards/eu-ai-act";
const publishedTime = "2026-02-15T00:00:00Z";
const modifiedTime = "2026-10-06T00:00:00Z";
const datesChecked = "2026-10-06";
const anchorLinks = [
  { href: "#critique", label: "Ethotechnics critique" },
  { href: "#failure-mode", label: "Failure mode" },
  { href: "#timeline", label: "Application dates" },
  { href: "#implementation-cluster", label: "Articles to clauses" },
  { href: "#implications-now", label: "Implications now" },
  { href: "#keep-current", label: "Sources and updates" },
];
const publication = derivePublicationMetadata({
  permalink,
  published: publishedTime,
  updated: modifiedTime,
  changelogSummary:
    "Listed the application dates from Article 113 as amended by the Digital Omnibus on AI, Regulation (EU) 2026/1744, which moved the Annex III high-risk date to 2 December 2027 and the Annex I date to 2 August 2028. Removed the overview of the Act.",
});

// Article 113 of Regulation (EU) 2024/1689, as amended by Regulation (EU)
// 2026/1744, checked against the Commission's AI Act Service Desk timeline.
const applicationDates = [
  { date: "1 August 2024", applies: "Entry into force." },
  {
    date: "2 February 2025",
    applies:
      "Chapters I and II: general provisions, including AI literacy (Art. 4), and the prohibited practices in Art. 5.",
  },
  {
    date: "2 August 2025",
    applies:
      "Obligations for general-purpose AI models (Chapter V), governance (Chapter VII), penalties (Chapter XII, except Art. 101), and notified bodies (Chapter III, Section 4).",
  },
  {
    date: "2 August 2026",
    applies:
      "The general application date: most remaining provisions, including the transparency obligations in Art. 50.",
  },
  {
    date: "2 December 2026",
    applies:
      "The prohibitions the omnibus added: AI systems that generate non-consensual intimate imagery or child sexual abuse material. The Art. 50(2) transition ends for certain providers of systems that generate synthetic content and were on the market before 2 August 2026.",
  },
  {
    date: "2 December 2027",
    applies:
      "Chapter III, Sections 1 to 3, for high-risk systems under Art. 6(2) and Annex III. The original date was 2 August 2026.",
  },
  {
    date: "2 August 2028",
    applies:
      "Chapter III, Sections 1 to 3, for high-risk systems under Art. 6(1) and Annex I, AI in products covered by EU harmonisation legislation. The original date was 2 August 2027.",
  },
];
---

<StandardsLayout
  title={pageTitle}
  description={pageDescription}
  permalink={permalink}
  seoTitle="EU AI Act: critique and implementation controls — Standards"
  eyebrow="Regulatory regime"
  anchorLinks={anchorLinks}
  lastUpdated={datesChecked}
  publishedTime={publishedTime}
  modifiedTime={modifiedTime}
>
  <SectionBlock
    id="critique"
    eyebrow="Ethotechnics critique"
    title="Where the regime falls short"
    description="The Act requires assessment before release and monitoring after it (Art. 72). The gaps are in what the running system owes the people it decides about."
    variant="alt"
  >
    <ul class="card__list">
      <li>
        <strong>Self-assessed authority does not expire.</strong> Most high-risk systems
        listed in Annex III are assessed by their own provider before release (Art.
        43(2)), and assessed again only after a substantial modification (Art. 43(4)).
        A notified body's certificate lapses after at most four or five years (Art.
        44(2)); a provider's own assessment does not, and nothing requires it to be
        renewed on evidence.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A challenge does not have to reach the system.</strong> A person affected
        by a high-risk system's decision can ask the deployer to explain it (Art.
        86), and anyone can complain to a market surveillance authority that the Act
        was breached (Art. 85). The Act gives no route to contest the decision itself;
        that comes from other law, such as GDPR Article 22. No provision in either
        ties a contest that succeeds to a change in the system.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong
          >Cost to people is assessed before use, not counted after.</strong
        >
        Public bodies, private entities providing public services, and deployers that
        score credit or price life and health insurance assess the impact on fundamental
        rights before first use, and update the assessment when its elements change
        (Art. 27). Nothing requires a measure of what the running system costs the
        people it decides about, such as the time they spend correcting its errors.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A halt is required; rehearsing it is not.</strong> Human oversight
        must, as appropriate and proportionate, let a person interrupt a high-risk
        system so it halts in a safe state (Art. 14(4)(e)). Nothing requires anyone
        to rehearse the halt or meet a recovery deadline.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </SectionBlock>

  <SectionBlock
    id="failure-mode"
    eyebrow="Failure mode"
    title="Lawful deployment + harmful latency"
    description="Compliance can coexist with prolonged pending states and slow restoration."
  >
    <p>
      The failure mode is a system that is compliant yet still traps people in
      extended “pending” states without enforceable restoration timelines.
    </p>
  </SectionBlock>

  <SectionBlock
    id="timeline"
    eyebrow="Application dates"
    title="When each part applies"
    description="Article 113, as amended by Regulation (EU) 2026/1744, the Digital Omnibus on AI, in force since 27 July 2026."
    variant="alt"
  >
    <div class="mapping-table__wrapper">
      <table class="mapping-table mapping-table--stack">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Date</th>
            <th scope="col">What applies</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {
            applicationDates.map((row) => (
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">{row.date}</th>
                <td data-label="What applies">{row.applies}</td>
              </tr>
            ))
          }
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="muted">
      Checked on {datesChecked} against the Commission's <a
        href="https://ai-act-service-desk.ec.europa.eu/en/ai-act/timeline/timeline-implementation-eu-ai-act"
        >AI Act Service Desk timeline</a
      >, which reflects the omnibus amendments.
    </p>
  </SectionBlock>

  <SectionBlock
    id="implementation-cluster"
    eyebrow="Articles to clauses"
    title="From the articles to the standards"
    description="The framework map takes Articles 9, 12, 14, 72, and 73 one at a time: the Ethotechnics clauses that bear on each, and what they leave to you."
  >
    <p>
      No clause covers serious-incident reporting under Article 73. <a
        href="/standards/enforceable-governance-crosswalks#eu-ai-act-articles"
        >Read the article table</a
      >.
    </p>
  </SectionBlock>

  <SectionBlock
    id="implications-now"
    eyebrow="Implications"
    title="What organizations should optimize for right now"
    description="Prepare for converging governance expectations, not a single date bet."
    variant="alt"
  >
    <ul class="card__list">
      <li>
        <strong>Prioritize auditability primitives:</strong> receipts, decision records,
        evidence packs, and repair logs that survive policy edits.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Operationalize time bounds:</strong> define and test time-to-human,
        time-to-remedy, and escalation paths with named authority.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Design for contestability:</strong> include standing, non-retaliation,
        and reliable recourse pathways that can be verified in an audit.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Crosswalk controls:</strong> map one control library across the EU
        AI Act, NIST AI RMF, and ISO/IEC 42001 so readiness does not hinge on one
        regime.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </SectionBlock>

  <SectionBlock
    id="keep-current"
    eyebrow="Maintenance"
    title="Sources and updates"
  >
    <ul class="card__list">
      <li>
        <strong>Sources:</strong> Article 113 of <a
          href="https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2024/1689/oj/eng"
          >Regulation (EU) 2024/1689</a
        > as amended by <a
          href="https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=OJ:L_202601744"
          >Regulation (EU) 2026/1744</a
        >, and the Commission's AI Act Service Desk timeline.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Updates:</strong> the page changes when a date, an obligation's scope,
        or an enforcement mechanism changes. The date it was last checked is at the
        top of the page.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </SectionBlock>
  <CitationBlock
    title={pageTitle}
    permalink={permalink}
    publication={publication}
  />
</StandardsLayout>
